(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-asthma-inhalers-status-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Asthma Steps, Inhaler Devices & Status Asthmaticus Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In the status asthmaticus algorithm, which route is preferred for steroids?","choices":["Inhaled","Intravenous","Oral","Topical"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The status asthmaticus algorithm lists steroids with the intravenous route preferred, alongside continuous nebulized albuterol and magnesium. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"How long should a patient wait between puffs of an inhaled quick-relief beta-2 agonist?","choices":["5 seconds","10 seconds","15 to 30 seconds","60 to 90 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For inhaled quick-relief medicines (beta-2 agonists), the patient waits 15 to 30 seconds between puffs. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"In the assess, adjust and review response cycle of asthma management, which item is checked when reviewing the response?","choices":["Confirmation of the diagnosis","Education and skills training","Inhaler technique and adherence","Exacerbations and side effects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reviewing the response covers symptoms, exacerbations, side effects, lung function and patient satisfaction, and the cycle then returns to assessment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What is the first step in using a metered dose inhaler?","choices":["Breathe out fully","Take off the cap and shake it","Press down while breathing in","Hold the breath for 10 seconds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first step is to take off the cap and shake the inhaler (it may need to be primed); the patient then breathes out all the way before holding the inhaler in place. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Which of these is listed as a reliever in stepwise asthma therapy?","choices":["Daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid on its own","Daily leukotriene receptor antagonist by mouth","Daily low-dose oral corticosteroid tablet","As-needed low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-formoterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As-needed low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-formoterol is the preferred reliever at every step (at steps 1 and 2 it is also a preferred controller); an as-needed short-acting beta-2 agonist is the other reliever option. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which inhaled delivery method requires the most coordination for proper use?","choices":["Dry powder inhaler","Nebulizer","Metered dose inhaler","Inhaler with a spacer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The metered dose inhaler requires the most coordination for proper use; a spacer holds the medication for patients with poor coordination. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Ketamine, listed for the intubated patient in status asthmaticus, belongs to which drug class?","choices":["Methylxanthine","Dissociative anesthetic","Anticholinergic","Mast cell stabilizer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic and an N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor antagonist; it increases catecholamine release, causing bronchodilation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which description defines status asthmaticus?","choices":["Attack unresponsive to first bronchodilators","Daily symptoms with weekly night waking","Bronchospasm provoked by cold air","Early reaction followed by a late reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Status asthmaticus is a medical emergency: an acute exacerbation that remains unresponsive to initial bronchodilator treatment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which mineral is part of the status asthmaticus algorithm, given as a bolus followed by a continuous infusion?","choices":["Magnesium","Potassium","Calcium","Sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Magnesium, a bolus followed by a continuous infusion, is part of the status asthmaticus algorithm with continuous nebulized albuterol and intravenous steroids. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"After breathing in the medication, how long should the patient hold the breath, if comfortable?","choices":["2 seconds","5 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The patient holds the breath for 10 seconds, or as long as is comfortable, after breathing in slowly and deeply. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How should long-term controller medications be used in asthma when they are indicated?","choices":["Every day","During attacks","Before exercise","When albuterol fails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is a chronic disease, and long-term controller medications need to be used daily if indicated; albuterol is the one for short-term relief. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A patient on low-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist still has poorly controlled asthma. Which preferred controller comes next?","choices":["High-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist with omalizumab","Daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid on its own","As-needed short-acting beta-2 agonist on its own","Medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid-long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The step after the low-dose combination (step 3) is the medium-dose combination (step 4); biologics come only after high-dose therapy and referral. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which device is a poor choice for a patient with poor inspiratory capacity?","choices":["Dry powder inhaler","Nebulizer","Metered dose inhaler","Valve holding chamber"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A dry powder inhaler needs the patient's inspiratory force to draw the powder out, so it is not good for patients with poor inspiratory capacity. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What distinguishes a valve holding chamber from a simple spacer?","choices":["An open tube extending the inhaler away","One-way valves that block exhaling into it","A compressor that makes a fine mist","Powdered drug drawn out by inhaling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A valve holding chamber has one-way valves that do not allow the patient to exhale into the device; a spacer is simply an open tube extending the inhaler away from the mouth. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 95"}]};
